  COMPARE `tømrer`: 166 ads.
  Even at the 95% UPPER bound the count is below 166. §13's inequality REVERSES,
  and its 'scarcity is not the mechanism' conclusion is retracted.

=== IS `explicitly_not_required` WHAT ITS NAME SAYS? ===

  93 ads carry the level. 82 contain NO explicit negation of Norwegian.
  Of those, 81 have an evidence span mentioning English.
  The census inferred the absence from a POSITIVE English requirement — defensible,
  but not what the level's name asserts. Ads that DO explicitly negate Norwegian
  ('an advantage, but not a requirement') are labelled `desirable` instead.

=== THE MECHANISM: do the accessible ads even DISCUSS Norwegian? ===

  accessible (not required)       63/   93 mention norsk/norwegian ( 67.7%)
  DEMANDING Norwegian           5525/ 5703 mention norsk/norwegian ( 96.9%)

  The seeker negates NORWEGIAN. The ad that would serve them affirms ENGLISH, and a
  third of the time never says `norsk` at all. The query's own `norsk` token pulls it
  toward the ads that discuss Norwegian — the blocking ones. That is cell 3's result
  (stating the constraint made it WORSE on 5 of 5) with a mechanism attached.

=== DISCRIMINATION: 93 accessible vs 5,703 demanding, AUC ===
    0.50 = indistinguishable.  BELOW 0.50 = prefers the blocking ads.

  query                                            AUC
  NO positive — Jeg snakker flytende norsk.      0.441
  NO negated  — Jeg snakker ikke norsk.          0.393
  EN positive — I speak fluent Norwegian.        0.426
  EN negated  — I do not speak Norwegian.        0.386
  english-positive                               0.297

  PAIRED DELTA (the controlled quantity — topic held fixed, only the polarity moves):
    NO  negating the language claim moves AUC -0.048
    EN  negating the language claim moves AUC -0.040

  Absolute AUC below 0.5 is NOT clean evidence on its own: the 93 skew academic and
  offshore while the 5,703 skew health and retail, and a one-line query carries no
  occupation signal to control that. The delta is the interpretable number.

written reports/absence_signal_adjudication.json

=== WHAT THIS SETTLES ===
  1. THE PATTERN OVER-FIRES; THE CENSUS DOES NOT UNDER-FIRE. §13's 334 is retracted,
     and with it §13's claim that document-side scarcity is not the mechanism.
  2. `explicitly_not_required` is a misnomer: 82 of 93 contain no explicit negation.
     The level is inferred from positive English requirements.
  3. The genuine negations sit in `desirable`, so the taxonomy boundary between
     `desirable` and `explicitly_not_required` is doing real work on §3b's 9.4%.
  4. STILL OPEN: whether `desirable` ads that say 'Norwegian not required' should count
     as accessible. That is a labelling decision, it is unmade, and it moves the
     headline number.
